== Applications to ideals in a polynomial ring <sec:polynomial-ideals>

Let us apply the local and global duality theorems for residues (see
§§@ss:local-duality[nn], @ss:global-duality[nn]) to the problem of whether a
given function belongs to the ideal $I(f)$ generated by a system of holomorphic
functions $f$ in the ring $CC[z] = CC[z_1, ..., z_n]$ if $h$ and $f$ are
polynomials and in the ring $scr(O)_a$ if $h$ and $f$ are defined locally.

#subsection[Macaulay's theorem] <ss:macaulay>
#index-mark(
  "Theorem",
  sub: "of Macaulay",
  target: <ss:macaulay>,
)Local duality works most transparently in the proof of a well-known theorem of
Macaulay. Let $f = (f_1, ..., f_n)$ be a system of homogeneous polynomials in
$CC^n$ (homogeneous polynomials will be called forms). Let us consider the
question of whether a given form $h$ belongs to the ideal $I(f)$, that is, the
question of the representability $h = q_1 f_1 + dots.c + q_n f_n$,
$q_j in CC[z]$.

#theorem[
  If a system of forms $f$ has an isolated zero at the origin, then any form $h$
  of degree greater than $deg f_1 + dots.c + deg f_n - n$ belongs to the ideal
  $I(f)$.
] <th:macaulay>

#proof[
  In the proof we use the following assertion.
]

#lemma[
  If $scr(P)$ and $scr(Q)$ are forms in $CC^n$ of degrees $p$ and $q$
  respectively and $p + n != q$, then the differential form
  $omega = scr(P) (z) d z \/ scr(Q) (z)$ is exact.
] <lem:exact-form>

To prove the lemma it is sufficient to observe that
$omega = (p + n - q)^(-1) d sigma$, where
$
  sigma = frac(scr(P), scr(Q)) sum_(j = 1)^n (-1)^(j - 1) z_j
  d z_1 and dots.c [j] dots.c and d z_n;
$
#source(162)here we need to use a formula of Euler, according to which
$
  sum z_j scr(P)'_(z_j) = p scr(P), wide sum z_j scr(Q)'_(z_j) = q scr(Q).
$

#proof(title: [Proof of the theorem.])[
  To prove the theorem we consider the local residue at zero
  $
    res_0 ""_f (h dot z^beta) = (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_Gamma
    frac(h z^beta d z, f_1 dots.c f_n), wide beta in NN^n.
  $
  From the condition on the degree of $h$ it follows that in the integrand the
  degree of the numerator is greater than the degree of the denominator minus
  $n$. By the lemma, we conclude that $res_0 ""_f (h z^beta) = 0$,
  $beta in NN^n$, so by the local duality theorem $h in I_0 (f)$, that is,
  $h equiv q_1 f_1 + dots.c + q_n f_n$, $q_j in scr(O)$. From the fact that
  $f_j$ and $h$ are homogeneous it is easy to conclude that the latter identity
  also holds with polynomial forms $q_j$ and even with forms $q_j$ such that
  $deg q_j + deg f_j = deg h$ for all $j$.
]

#subsection[Noether's theorem in $PP_n$] <ss:noether>
#index-mark(
  "Theorem",
  sub: "of Noether",
  target: <ss:noether>,
)Suppose that the system of polynomials $f = (f_1, ..., f_n)$ has a discrete set
of zeros $Z_f$ in $CC^n$ and that $I(f)$ is the ideal generated by the system
$f$ in the ring $CC[z]$. According to the Lasker-Noether theorem (see
@ss:ideals), for a given polynomial $h$ we have
$
  h = q_1 f_1 + dots.c + q_n f_n
$ <eq:noether-identity>
with polynomials $q_j$ (that is, $h in I(f)$) if at each point $a in Z_f$ the
local condition of Noether is satisfied:
$
  h in I_a (f).
$
#new-par
We prove that in the case when the system $f$ does not have zeros at infinity in
projective space, Noether's local conditions ensure the identity
@eq:noether-identity with $q_j$ such that
$
  deg q_j + deg f_j <= deg h, wide j = 1, ..., n.
$ <eq:degree-restrictions>
Let us restate this fact in the language of homogeneous polynomials in
projective space $PP_n$. For this, with each polynomial $g(z)$ we associate its
"homogenization"
$tilde(g) (xi) = xi_0^(deg g) g(xi_1 \/ xi_0, ..., xi_n \/ xi_0)$ and
conversely, with each homogeneous polynomial $tilde(g) (xi)$ we put into
correspondence the polynomial $g(z) = tilde(g) (1, z_1, ..., z_n)$. Then the
identity @eq:noether-identity with the restrictions @eq:degree-restrictions on
the degrees is equivalent to the fact that there are homogeneous polynomials
$tilde(q)_j$ such that
$tilde(h) = tilde(q)_1 tilde(f)_1 + dots.c + tilde(q)_n tilde(f)_n$.

Thus, let $xi = (xi_0, xi_1, ..., xi_n)$ be homogeneous coordinates of the
projective space $PP_n$, and let
$tilde(f) = (tilde(f)_1 (xi), ..., tilde(f)_n (xi))$ be a system of forms that
has a discrete set of zeros $Z_(tilde(f))$ in $PP_n$. For each
$a in Z_(tilde(f))$ (suppose that $a = (a_0, a_1, ..., a_n)$ and for
definiteness that $a_0 != 0$) we denote by $I_a (f)$ the ideal in the ring
$scr(O)_a$ of holomorphic germs in the variables $z_j = xi_j \/ xi_0$, generated
by the system $f = (f_1 (z), ..., f_n (z))$, where the $f_j$ are obtained from
the $tilde(f)_j$ by passing to local coordinates $z$ in the way described above.

#theorem[
  #source(163)Let $tilde(f) = (tilde(f)_1 (xi), ..., tilde(f)_n (xi))$ be a
  system of forms with a finite set of common zeros $Z_(tilde(f))$ in the
  projective space $PP_n$ and $tilde(h) (xi)$ be a certain form. If, in the
  local coordinates $z$ at each point $a in Z_(tilde(f))$, Noether's local
  condition $h in I_a (f)$ is satisfied then $tilde(h) in I(tilde(f))$, that is,
  there are forms $tilde(q)_j$ such that
  $
    tilde(h) (xi) = tilde(q)_1 (xi) tilde(f)_1 (xi) + dots.c
    + tilde(q)_n (xi) tilde(f)_n (xi),
  $ <eq:homogeneous-identity>
  where $deg tilde(q)_j + deg tilde(f)_j = deg tilde(h)$ for all $j$ from 1 to
  $n$.
] <th:noether>

Before proving the theorem, we establish a connection between the residues of
rational differential forms of degree $n$ in $PP_n$ and residues of forms of
degree $n + 1$ in $CC^(n + 1)$. Let $pi: CC^(n + 1) -> PP_n$ be a canonical
mapping: $pi(xi) = [xi]$. Usually a mapping $f: X -> Y$ of smooth manifolds $X$
and $Y$ induces a homomorphism $f^\#: scr(E)^p (Y) -> scr(E)^p (X)$ of spaces of
differential forms of the same degree and a homomorphism
$f_\#: C_p (X) -> C_p (Y)$ of groups of chains also of the same dimension. With
the mapping $pi$ there are associated two other homomorphisms. The first is the
homomorphism $pi^(>): Omega_r^n (PP_n) -> Omega_r^(n + 1) (CC^(n + 1))$ of
spaces of rational differential forms of degree $n$ in $PP_n$ and degree $n + 1$
in $CC^(n + 1)$. Its construction is as follows. It is known (see
@bib:Griffiths1969) that in $PP_n$ any rational differential form $omega$ of
degree $n$ is written in homogeneous coordinates $xi$ in the form
$scr(P) (xi) sigma(xi) \/ scr(Q) (xi)$, where
$
  sigma(xi) = sum_(k = 0)^n (-1)^k xi_k d xi_0 and dots.c [k] dots.c and d xi_n,
$ <eq:projective-sigma>
and $scr(P) (xi)$ and $scr(Q) (xi)$ are homogeneous polynomials with the
following relation between their degrees: $deg scr(P) + n + 1 = deg scr(Q)$.
Then $pi^(>) (omega) = scr(P) (xi) d xi \/ scr(Q) (xi)$, where the $xi$ are
regarded as local coordinates of the space $CC^(n + 1)$.

The second homomorphism associated with $pi$ is the homomorphism
$pi_(>): C_n (PP_n) -> C_(n + 1) (CC^(n + 1))$ of groups of chains, which is
defined as follows. For any singular $n$-simplex $[tau]: Delta -> PP_n$ we
define a singular $(n + 1)$-chain $pi_(>) [tau]$ in $CC^(n + 1)$ by the
following action on the cell $Delta times [0, 1]$:
$
  pi_(>) [tau] (t, theta) = frac(tau(t), |tau(t)|) e^(-2 pi i theta),
  wide t in Delta, quad theta in [0, 1];
$
as usual, we assume here that $tau(t)$ is the representative in $CC^(n + 1)$ of
the point $[tau(t)] in PP_n$. In the language of supports of chains, $pi_(>)$
admits the following interpretation. The space $PP_n$ with homogeneous
coordinates $xi$ and the space $CC^(n + 1)$ with coordinates $xi$ can be
regarded as the infinite and affine parts of the projective space
$PP_(n + 1) = PP_n union CC^(n + 1)$. To each point $[a] in PP_n$ there
corresponds the complex ray $l_a = {lambda a: lambda in CC}$ in $CC^(n + 1)$
whose closure in $PP_(n + 1)$ "pierces" the infinite part $PP_n$ at the point
$[a]$. Now in order to describe the support of the chain $pi_(>) [tau]$ it is
necessary to construct, for each point $[a]$ of the #source(164)support
$|[tau]|$, a circle of unit radius in $l_a$ and take the union of the circles at
all points $[a] in |[tau]|$. We have thus shown that $pi_(>)$ coincides with the
Leray coboundary operator (see @bib:Leray1959)
$delta: C_n (PP_n) -> C_(n + 1) (PP_(n + 1) without PP_n)$.

We can now describe the connection between residues in $PP_n$ and $CC^(n + 1)$.

#proposition[
  For any rational differential $n$-form $omega = scr(P) sigma \/ scr(Q)$ in
  $PP_n$ and any compact $n$-chain $Gamma$ in
  $PP_n without {[xi]: scr(Q) (xi) = 0}$ we have
  $
    (-1)^(n + 1) 2 pi i integral_Gamma omega
    = integral_(pi_(>) Gamma) pi^(>) (omega).
  $ <eq:projective-residue-relation>
] <prop:projective-residues>

#proof[
  The extension of the form $pi^(>) (omega) = scr(P) d xi \/ scr(Q)$ to the
  projective compactification $PP_(n + 1)$ of the space $CC^(n + 1)$ has the
  hyperplane at infinity ${xi_(n + 1) = 0} tilde.eq PP_n$ (we assume that the
  $xi$ are the homogeneous coordinates of $PP_n$, and $(xi, xi_(n + 1))$ are the
  homogeneous coordinates of $PP_(n + 1)$) as a pole of the first order. More
  precisely, in each coordinate neighborhood
  $scr(U)_k = {[xi, xi_(n + 1)]: xi_k != 0}$, $k = 0, 1, ..., n$, of the
  manifold $PP_(n + 1)$, with $xi_0, ..., [k], ..., xi_(n + 1)$ as local
  coordinates, the form $pi^(>) (omega)$ is given by
  $
    pi^(>) (omega) = (-1)^(n - k + 1) frac(
      scr(P) (xi_0, ..., 1, ..., xi_n),
      scr(Q) (xi_0, ..., limits(1)_(hat(k)), ..., xi_n),
    ) d xi_0 and dots.c [k] dots.c and d xi_n and
    frac(d xi_(n + 1), xi_(n + 1)).
  $ <eq:projective-extended-form>
  In a similar coordinate neighborhood $tilde(scr(U))_k = {[xi]: xi_k != 0}$ of
  the manifold $PP_n$ the form $omega$ is written as follows:
  $
    omega = (-1)^k frac(
      scr(P) (xi_0, ..., 1, ..., xi_n),
      scr(Q) (xi_0, ..., limits(1)_(hat(k)), ..., xi_n),
    ) d xi_0 and dots.c [k] dots.c and d xi_n.
  $ <eq:projective-local-form>
  Thus, in a neighborhood of the submanifold
  $PP_n without {[xi]: scr(Q) (xi) = 0}$ of the manifold $PP_(n + 1)$ the forms
  @eq:projective-extended-form and @eq:projective-local-form are connected by
  the relation
  $pi^(>) (omega) = (-1)^(n + 1) omega and (d xi_(n + 1) \/ xi_(n + 1))$.
  Therefore, taking into account the interpretation of $pi_(>)$ as the
  coboundary operator $delta$, the equality @eq:projective-residue-relation
  follows from Leray's formula.#ed-note[The 1992 edition has no signs
    $(-1)^(n + 1)$, $(-1)^(n - k + 1)$ and $(-1)^k$ in
    @eq:projective-residue-relation–@eq:projective-local-form and in the
    relation between @eq:projective-extended-form and @eq:projective-local-form;
    with them the formulas hold for the orientation of the cell
    $Delta times [0, 1]$ as a product. With this orientation
    $pi_(>) tilde(Gamma)$ in the proof of the theorem below is homologous to
    $(-1)^(n + 1) Gamma_0 (F)$; the integrals there vanish, so the argument is
    not affected.]
]

#proof(title: [Proof of the theorem.])[
  We choose a system of homogeneous coordinates in $PP_n$ so that the hyperplane
  ${xi_0 = 0}$ does not contain points of the discrete set $Z_(tilde(f))$. Then
  for all points $a in Z_(tilde(f))$ we can introduce a unique system of local
  coordinates $z_1 = xi_1 \/ xi_0$, ..., $z_n = xi_n \/ xi_0$. We denote the
  degree of the polynomial $tilde(f)_i$ by $d_i$ and consider the following
  $n$-dimensional cycle in $PP_n$:
  $
    tilde(Gamma) = {[xi] in PP_n: |tilde(f)_i (xi)| = epsilon abs(xi_0)^(d_i),
      quad i = 1, ..., n}.
  $
  For sufficiently small $epsilon$ this cycle splits into the sum
  $tilde(Gamma) = sum_(a in Z_(tilde(f))) tilde(Gamma)_a$ of cycles of the form
  $
    tilde(Gamma)_a = {[xi] in scr(U)_a: |tilde(f)_i (xi)| = epsilon
      abs(xi_0)^(d_i), quad i = 1, ..., n};
  $
  here $scr(U)_a$ are disjoint neighborhoods of the points $a in Z_(tilde(f))$.
  The cycles $tilde(Gamma)_a$ in local coordinates $z$ are given in the form
  $
    tilde(Gamma)_a = Gamma_a = {z in scr(U)_a: |f_i (z)| = epsilon,
      quad i = 1, ..., n},
  $
  #source(165)where $f_i (z) = tilde(f)_i (1, z)$. Consequently,
  $Gamma_a = Gamma_a^epsilon (f)$ is a local cycle at the point $a$, associated
  with the mapping $f$. By hypothesis, $h in I_a (f)$ for all $a in Z_f$, so
  from the proposition of @ss:integral-definition for any form $tilde(g) (xi)$
  of degree $d_1 + dots.c + d_n - n$ we have
  $
    (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_(tilde(Gamma)) frac(
      tilde(h) (xi) tilde(g) (xi) sigma(xi),
      xi_0^(d + 1) tilde(f)_1 (xi) dots.c tilde(f)_n (xi),
    ) = sum_(a in Z_f) res_a ""_f (h g) = 0,
  $ <eq:projective-residue-sum>
  where $d$ is the degree of $tilde(h)$ and $sigma$ is the differential form
  @eq:projective-sigma. We apply the result of the proposition to the integral
  on the left-hand side, first observing that the cycle
  $
    pi_(>) tilde(Gamma) ~ delta tilde(Gamma) = {[xi, xi_(n + 1)]:
      |xi_(n + 1)| = tau abs(xi_0),
      quad |tilde(f)_i (xi)| = epsilon abs(xi_0)^(d_i), quad i = 1, ..., n}
  $
  in local coordinates $xi$ of the space $CC^(n + 1) subset PP_(n + 1)$ is given
  in the form
  $
    pi_(>) tilde(Gamma) ~ {xi in CC^(n + 1): |xi_0| = 1 \/ tau,
      quad |tilde(f)_i (xi)| = epsilon \/ tau^(d_i), quad i = 1, ..., n}.
  $
  Thus, $pi_(>) tilde(Gamma)$ is homologous to the local cycle $Gamma_0 (F)$ at
  the point $0 in CC^(n + 1)$ associated with the mapping
  $F = (xi_0^(d + 1), tilde(f)_1, ..., tilde(f)_n)$. Consequently, by formulas
  @eq:projective-residue-relation and @eq:projective-residue-sum
  $
    res_0 ""_F (tilde(h) tilde(g)) = (2 pi i)^(-n - 1)
    integral_(pi_(>) tilde(Gamma)) frac(
      tilde(h) (xi) tilde(g) (xi) d xi,
      xi_0^(d + 1) tilde(f)_1 (xi) dots.c tilde(f)_n (xi),
    ) = 0
  $
  for any form $tilde(g) (xi)$ of degree $d_1 + dots.c + d_n - n$. From this and
  the lemma of @ss:macaulay[subsection] it follows that the residue
  $res_0 ""_F (tilde(h) tilde(g))$ is equal to zero for all
  $tilde(g) in scr(O)_0$. Therefore by the local duality theorem we have
  $
    tilde(h) = tilde(q)_0 xi_0^(d + 1) + tilde(q)_1 tilde(f)_1 + dots.c
    + tilde(q)_n tilde(f)_n, wide tilde(q)_j in scr(O)_0.
  $
  Now from the homogeneity of the polynomials $tilde(h)$, $tilde(f)_1$, ...,
  $tilde(f)_n$ and the equality $d = deg tilde(h)$ we obtain
  @eq:homogeneous-identity.
]

#remark[
  @th:noether[The theorem] is not true for overdetermined systems. For example,
  let $z = (z_1, z_2)$, $f = (f_1 (z), f_2 (z), f_3 (z))$, where $f_1 = z_1$,
  $f_2 = z_2 - z_1^2$, $f_3 = z_1^2 + z_2^2$. The system $f$ has a unique zero
  $z = 0$ in projective space. We have $h(z) = z_2 in I(f)$, since
  $z_2 = z_1 f_1 + f_2$, but it is easy to see that there is no representation
  $h = q_1 f_1 + q_2 f_2 + q_3 f_3$ with restrictions on the degree of the form
  @eq:degree-restrictions. In an equivalent formulation this implies that for
  the forms $tilde(h) = xi_2$, $tilde(f)_1 = xi_1$,
  $tilde(f)_2 = xi_0 xi_2 - xi_1^2$, $tilde(f)_3 = xi_1^2 + xi_2^2$ the local
  Noether conditions are satisfied, but there is no identity of the form
  @eq:homogeneous-identity.
] <rem:noether-overdetermined>

#subsection[Verification of the local Noether conditions]
<ss:noether-conditions>
In order to apply the Lasker-Noether theorem (see @ss:ideals) or the Noether
theorem in $PP_n$ it is necessary to verify the local Noether conditions about
the inclusion $h in I_a (f)$. Let us give some criteria (sufficient conditions)
for such inclusions. There are many approaches to the achievement of sufficient
conditions, but they are #source(166)all far from necessary (obviously any
sufficient condition for the inclusion $h in I_a (f)$ that is necessary is so
"immense" that it turns into a "nonworking tautology"). Our approach consists in
finding for the system $f$ the smallest possible number $rho$ such that any
function $h$ with order $d_a (h) >= rho$ (for the definition of order, see
@ss:initial-terms) belongs to the ideal $I_a (f)$. Thus, we shall solve the
problem of searching for the smallest number $rho$ such that
$frak(m)^rho subset I_a (f)$, where $frak(m)$ is the maximal ideal in $scr(O)_a$
generated by the coordinate functions $z_1 - a_1$, ..., $z_n - a_n$. This number
$rho$ is none other than the order of the primary ideal $I_a (f)$ (see
@ss:ideals), since the prime ideal associated with it is the ideal $frak(m)$.
The absolute bound for $rho$ is
$
  rho <= mu;
$ <eq:order-absolute-bound>
here $mu = mu_a (f)$ is the multiplicity of the mapping $f$ at the point $a$. In
order to verify this we use the result of @ss:local-nullstellensatz, according
to which $l^mu in I_a (f)$ for any linear form
$l = c_1 (z_1 - a_1) + dots.c + c_n (z_n - a_n)$. Since the family of
homogeneous polynomials of the form $l^mu$, where $l$ runs over the entire set
of linear forms, generates the vector space of all homogeneous polynomials of
degree $mu$, it follows that $frak(m)^mu subset I_a (f)$; this proves the bound
@eq:order-absolute-bound.

The bound @eq:order-absolute-bound is often rough, and it can be made more
precise if we have some information about the functions $f_j$ of the system $f$.
We recall that $(f_j)_*$ denotes the initial polynomial in the Taylor expansion
of the function $f_j$ at the point $a$ (see @ss:initial-terms).

#theorem[
  Let $f = (f_1, ..., f_n)$ be a system of holomorphic functions in some
  neighborhood of a point $a in CC^n$ and $d_j = deg (f_j)_*$ be the order of
  $f_j$ at $a$. If for some $j in {1, ..., n}$ the set of solutions of the
  system of equations $(f_1)_* = dots.c [j] dots.c = (f_n)_* = 0$ consists of
  finitely many rays in $CC^n$, then for the order $rho$ of the primary ideal
  $I_a (f)$ we have the bound
  $
    rho <= mu - d_1 dots.c d_n + d_1 + dots.c + d_n - n + 1.
  $ <eq:order-bound>
] <th:order-bound>

The bound @eq:order-bound is very much simplified if we impose more rigid
restrictions on the initial polynomials $(f_j)_*$ than in the theorem. Namely,
we assume that the system $(f)_* = ((f_1)_*, ..., (f_n)_*)$ of initial
polynomials has a unique zero at $a$. According to the theorem of
@ss:initial-terms, in this case $mu = d_1 dots.c d_n$ and for the order $rho$ we
obtain the bound
$
  rho <= d_1 + dots.c + d_n - n + 1,
$ <eq:order-bound-simple>
which is consistent with Macaulay's theorem when $f = (f)_*$ is a system of
homogeneous polynomials. The bound @eq:order-bound-simple is exact, because
under our assumptions the Jacobian $partial f \/ partial z$ does not belong to
the ideal $I_a (f)$ and has order at $a$ equal to $d_1 + dots.c + d_n - n$.

#proof(title: [Proof of the theorem.])[
  We shall assume that $a = 0$ and the set of solutions of the system of
  equations $(f_1)_* = dots.c = (f_(n - 1))_* = 0$ consists of finitely many
  rays in $CC^n$ (that is, we put the number $j$ mentioned in the #source(167)
  statement of the theorem equal to $n$). We denote the right-hand side of
  @eq:order-bound by $beta$. Then according to the local duality theorem it is
  sufficient to establish that
  $
    res_0 ""_f (z^alpha) = (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_(Gamma_a^epsilon (f))
    frac(z^alpha d z, f_1 (z) dots.c f_n (z)) = 0, wide abs(alpha) >= beta.
  $ <eq:monomial-residue>
  We can obtain a rotation of the coordinate system in $CC^n$ so that for the
  complex curve $K = {f' = 0}$ ($f'$ denotes the system $(f_1, ..., f_(n - 1))$)
  and its tangent cone $C(K) = {(f')_* = 0}$ the conditions
  $
    K inter {z_n = 0} = {0}, wide C(K) inter {z_n = 0} = {0}
  $ <eq:blowup-conditions>
  are satisfied at zero (see Lemma~@lem:tangent-cone of @ss:initial-terms). Then
  according to the lemma of @ss:resultant-multiplicity the local cycle
  $Gamma_a^epsilon (f)$ in @eq:monomial-residue can be replaced by the cycle
  $
    Gamma = {z in scr(U)_0: |f_j (z)| = epsilon, quad j = 1, ..., n - 1,
      quad |z_n| = delta}
  $
  homologous to it. In the integral @eq:monomial-residue we make the change of
  the variables $z = sigma(w)$: $z_j = w_j w_n$, $j = 1, ..., n - 1$,
  $z_n = w_n$ (for the reader who is familiar with the concept of #index-mark(
    "Monoidal transformation",
  )monoidal transformation (see @bib:Mumford1976), this implies transition to
  one of the systems of local coordinates $w_j = z_j \/ z_n$, $j != n$,
  $w_n = z_n$ of the manifold $tilde(scr(B))$ obtained from the polydisk
  $scr(B)$ by the monoidal transformation $sigma: tilde(scr(B)) -> scr(B)$ that
  blows up the origin $0 in scr(B)$ into the hyperplane $E = sigma^(-1) (0)$).
  As a result of this substitution the integral @eq:monomial-residue takes the
  form
  $
    res_0 ""_f (z^alpha) = (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_(Gamma^*) frac(
      w_1^(alpha_1) dots.c w_(n - 1)^(alpha_(n - 1)) w_n^(abs(alpha) - d),
      f_1^* (w) dots.c f_n^* (w),
    ) d w,
  $ <eq:blown-up-residue>
  where
  $
    f_j^* (w) = w_n^(-d_j) f_j (sigma(w)), wide d = d_1 + dots.c + d_n - n + 1,
    \
    Gamma^* = {w in sigma^(-1) (scr(U)_0): |f_j^* (w)| = epsilon_j,
      quad j = 1, ..., n - 1, quad |w_n| = delta}.
  $
  Since $f_j^* (w)$ has the form $(f_j)_* (w', 1) + w_n g_j (w)$ with $g_j$
  holomorphic, by the hypothesis of the theorem the system of equations
  $f^* (w) = 0$ has only finitely many roots on the hyperplane ${w_n = 0}$. We
  denote the number of these roots (with multiplicities) by $mu(f^*)$. Then
  under condition @eq:blowup-conditions the following assertion holds.
]

#lemma[
  We have $mu(f^*) = mu - d_1 dots.c d_n$, that is, under the monoidal
  transformation there is a loss of $d_1 dots.c d_n$ roots.
] <lem:lost-roots>

#proof[
  Let $p_nu^* (w_n) = (w'^((nu)) (w_n), w_n)$, $nu = 1, ..., r$, be all of the
  branches of the curve
  $
    K^* = {f'^* = 0} = overline(sigma^(-1) (K without {0})),
  $
  that intersect the hyperplane ${w_n = 0}$. We denote by $mu_nu$ the
  multiplicity of the branch $p_nu^*$, that is, the multiplicity of the zero
  $w'^((nu)) (w_n)$ of the mapping #source(168)$f'^*$. Then $mu(f^*)$ is equal
  to the multiplicity of the zero $w_n = 0$ of the resultant (see
  @ss:resultant-multiplicity)
  $
    R(w_n) = product_(nu = 1)^r [f_n^* (p_nu^*)]^(mu_nu)
    = w_n^(-d_n (mu_1 + dots.c + mu_r)) product_(nu = 1)^r
    [f_n (p_nu)]^(mu_nu),
  $
  where $p_nu = (w_n w'^((nu)) (w_n), w_n)$ (we observe that this resultant is
  equal to the product of the resultants constructed at each point of the
  intersection $K^* inter {w_n = 0}$). Recalling the dependence between the
  variables $z$ and $w$, it is easy to see that
  $p_nu (z_n) = (z_n w'^((nu)) (z_n), z_n)$, $nu = 1, ..., r$, are the branches
  of the curve $K$ at zero. Therefore
  $
    R^* (z_n) = z_n^(-d_n (mu_1 + dots.c + mu_r)) product_(nu = 1)^r
    [f_n (p_nu (z_n))]^(mu_nu),
  $ <eq:resultant-star>
  where $p_nu = p_nu (z_n)$, $nu = 1, ..., r$, are all of the branches of $K$ at
  zero. Since $sigma$ biholomorphically maps the complement
  $tilde(scr(B)) without E$ of the set $E supset {w_n = 0}$ onto the punctured
  polydisk $scr(B) without {0}$, $w'^((nu)) (z_n)$ and $z'^((nu)) (z_n)$ are the
  zeros of equal multiplicities of the mappings $f'^*$ and $f'$ (we recall that
  $sigma$ is such that $w_n = z_n$). According to Lemma~@lem:tangent-cone of
  @ss:initial-terms, the sum of the multiplicities $mu_nu$ is equal to
  $d_1 dots.c d_(n - 1)$. Thus, @eq:resultant-star implies that the resultant
  $R^*$ of the system $f^*$ and the resultant
  $
    R(z_n) = product_(nu = 1)^r [f_n (p_nu (z_n))]^(mu_nu)
  $
  of the system $f$ are connected by the relation
  $R^* (u) = u^(-d_1 dots.c d_n) R(u)$. Taking into account that the
  multiplicities of the zero $u = 0$ of the resultants $R^*$ and $R$ are equal
  to the number $mu(f^*)$ and the multiplicity $mu = mu_a (f)$ respectively, we
  obtain the assertion of the lemma.
]

Returning to the proof of the theorem, we denote the set of zeros of the system
$f^*$ that lie on ${w_n = 0}$ by ${a_1, ..., a_l}$. Since the sum of the
multiplicities $mu_(a_k) (f^*)$ of these zeros is equal to $mu(f^*)$, by the
lemma
$
  mu_(a_k) (f^*) <= mu - d_1 dots.c d_n.
$ <eq:blowup-multiplicities>
Next, for sufficiently small $epsilon_j$ and $delta$ the cycle $Gamma^*$ splits
into the sum of connected components close to the points $a_k$, so for the
integral @eq:blown-up-residue we have
$
  res_0 ""_f (z^alpha) = sum_(k = 1)^l res_(a_k) ""_(f^*)
  (w_1^(alpha_1) dots.c w_(n - 1)^(alpha_(n - 1)) w_n^(abs(alpha) - d)).
$ <eq:blowup-residue-split>
If $abs(alpha) >= beta$, then from @eq:blowup-multiplicities for each $k$ from 1
to $l$ we obtain $abs(alpha) - d >= mu_(a_k) (f^*)$. Hence according to the
absolute bound @eq:order-absolute-bound and the proposition of
@ss:integral-definition each residue in @eq:blowup-residue-split is equal to
zero, and the required equality @eq:monomial-residue is proved.

#subsection[Remark on global duality] <ss:global-duality-remark>
#source(169)The Lasker-Noether theorem reveals the connections between the
structure of the ideal $I(f) subset CC[z]$ and the geometry of the algebraic
sets ${f_j = 0}$ (here $f_j$ are the functions of the system $f$) at the zeros
of the system $f$. To verify the inclusion $h in I(f)$, there is no need to know
the zeros of the system $f$, of course. For example, by global duality (see
@ss:global-duality) the polynomial $h$ belongs to the ideal $I(f)$ if and only
if the global residue $Res_f (h g)$ is equal to zero for any polynomial $g$; but
as we know from @ss:global-transformation, the global residue can be calculated
without knowing the zeros of the system $f$. In addition, to verify the
inclusion $h in I(f)$ it is sufficient to calculate $Res_f (h g)$ only for
finitely many polynomials $g$. Let us show how we can choose these polynomials.
Let
$
  f_j (zeta) - f_j (z) = sum_(k = 1)^n (zeta_k - z_k) P_(j k) (zeta, z),
  wide j = 1, ..., n,
$
be the Hefer expansions for the polynomials $f_j$ of the system $f$, and let
$H(zeta, z)$ be the determinant of the matrix $norm(P_(j k))$. This determinant
can be represented in the form
$
  H(zeta, z) = sum_(k = 1)^L chi_k (zeta) psi_k (z).
$ <eq:hefer-determinant-expansion>

#proposition[
  If the polynomial mapping $f = (f_1, ..., f_n): CC^n -> CC^n$ has a discrete
  set of zeros, then the polynomial $h$ belongs to the ideal $I(f)$ if and only
  if $Res_f (h psi_k) = 0$ for all $k$ from 1 to $L$, where $psi_k$ are the
  polynomials of the expansion @eq:hefer-determinant-expansion. If the mapping
  $w = f(z)$ is proper, then the family of polynomials ${psi_k}_(k = 1, ..., L)$
  generates the ring $CC[z]$, regarded as a module over the ring $CC[w]$, that
  is, each polynomial $g in CC[z]$ admits the expansion
  $
    g(z) = c_1 (w) psi_1 (z) + dots.c + c_L (w) psi_L (z), wide w = f(z).
  $
] <prop:finite-generators>

#proof[
  Let ${scr(U)_a}_(a in Z)$ be a family of disjoint neighborhoods of the points
  $a in Z = f^(-1) (0)$, and suppose that $r$ is so small that the polyhedra
  $Pi_a = Pi_a^r (f)$ are relatively compact in $scr(U)_a$. We set
  $Pi^r = union.big_(a in Z) Pi_a$, and let $Gamma^r$ be the distinguished
  boundary of $Pi^r$ (that is, the union of the distinguished boundaries of the
  polyhedra $Pi_a$). According to Weil's formula, for each $g in CC[z]$ we have
  $
    g(z) = (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_(Gamma^r)
    frac(g(zeta) H(zeta, z), f(zeta) - f(z)) d zeta, wide z in Pi^r.
  $
  Hence, taking account of @eq:hefer-determinant-expansion, we obtain
  $
    g(z) = sum_(k = 1)^L c_k (f(z)) psi_k (z), wide z in Pi^r,
  $ <eq:psi-expansion>
  #source(170)where the $c_k (w)$ are functions holomorphic in the polydisk
  $scr(B)_r = {w: |w_j| < r, j = 1, ..., n}$ defined by the integrals
  $
    c_k (w) = (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_(Gamma^r)
    frac(g(zeta) chi_k (zeta), f(zeta) - w) d zeta, wide w in scr(B)_r.
  $ <eq:psi-coefficients>
  Let $c_k (w) = sum_alpha c_(k alpha) w^alpha$. Since $f^alpha in I(f)$ for all
  $alpha != 0$, from @eq:psi-expansion and the proposition of
  @ss:integral-definition
  $
    Res_f (h g) = sum_(k = 1)^L c_(k 0) Res_f (h psi_k).
  $
  According to global duality, the first part of our assertion follows from
  this.

  If the mapping $w = f(z)$ is proper, then for any $w in CC_w^n$ the system
  $f - w$ has the same number of zeros, taken with multiplicities. From Rouché's
  principle it follows that for $w in scr(B)_r$ all of the zeros of this system
  lie in the polyhedron $Pi^r = {z in CC^n:
    |f_j (z)| < r, j = 1, ..., n}$, where $r$ is any positive real number.
  Consequently, the integral @eq:psi-coefficients expresses the total sum of the
  residues of the polynomial $g chi_k$ with respect to the mapping $f - w$.
  Hence by the theorem of @ss:global-transformation we conclude that the
  $c_k (w)$ are rational functions. In addition, these functions are entire,
  because by the lemma of @ss:boundary-representation the cycle $Gamma^r$ in
  @eq:psi-coefficients is homologous in the domain of regularity of the
  integrand to any cycle $Gamma^R$, $R > r$ (this implies the holomorphic
  extendability of $c_k (w)$ from $scr(B)_r$ to $scr(B)_R$). Thus, the $c_k$ are
  polynomials, and from @eq:psi-expansion we deduce that $CC[z]$ is a
  $CC[w]$-module with the system of generators ${psi_k (z)}_(k = 1, ..., L)$.
]
